      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2014 Institute of Pharmacology, Polish Academy of Sciences. Published by Elsevier Urban & Partner Sp. z o.o. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Recent studies have shown that influence of antidepressants on analgesic action of opioids is heterogeneous. The aim of this study was to investigate the effect of acute and repeated (21 days) antidepressant (amitriptyline, moclobemide and reboxetine) treatment on the antinociceptive action of morphine, an opioid agonist, in streptozotocin (STZ)-induced neuropathic pain model."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "The studies were performed on the male Wistar rats. The changes in nociceptive thresholds were determined by using mechanical stimuli (the Randall-Selitto and the von Frey tests). Diabetes was induced by intramuscular administration of STZ."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "In this work we report that acute as well as repeated per os administration of antidepressants (amitriptyline, moclobemide and reboxetine) significantly potentiated the antihyperalgesic effect of morphine in STZ-induced neuropathic pain model."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Combination therapy, such as classical antidepressants (amitriptyline, moclobemide) with opioids, or agents with noradrenaline reuptake inhibition and μ-opioid receptor activation could be a new target for research into treatment of painful diabetic neuropathy."
          }
        ]
      },
